7a27383201bc352e2ec6a383444a","position":"1786992515691411358-a80fa9a1-ec50-49ff-938f-ebe7cde8bc7a"},"signature":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":"ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIHe3p+OG3L79IIh99qRBEkAEXaU+VbeQcSfhIu4M2F8b rebar-bot joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com"}],"bridge_alerts":[],"bridge_project":null,"claim_harness":null,"claim_remote_session":null,"claimed_session":null,"comments":[{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"RESEARCH → DRAFT gate: the dependency findings, probes, and research critique were completed on relevant-liberal-xantus and in the three RP-04 recovery artifacts; the maintainer explicitly approved the recommendation and authorized this ADR draft. This task remains documentation-only and will stop before HOLD/MERGE for a separate explicit approval.","timestamp":1786991171053065001},{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"Tracker lifecycle correction: parent-first claim makes an open child unclaimable until its parent epic is plan-reviewed and started. That would violate the approved sequencing (ADR first; implementation graph, epic review, and epic claim later). This ADR is therefore modeled as a standalone prerequisite that blocks vibrant-legal-hind, rather than as a contained implementation child. Its scope and acceptance criteria are unchanged; the relationship now enforces the intended order.","timestamp":1786991507885666001},{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"# RP-04 ADR 0098 — Draft Critique\n\nDate: 2026-08-17\nADR task: `miniscule-unschooled-snowmonkey` (`23e9-cad1-64bf-4bb7`)\nEpic: `vibrant-legal-hind` (`e215-ab23-ed6a-4791`)\nDraft commit: `a72b59695ea973e04e892292233aefee843bff31`\nGerrit change: <https://rebar.solutions.navateam.com/c/rebar/+/1840> (WIP)\n\n## 1. Claims that do not cite a research finding\n\nNo unresolved external-dependency claim was found. Provider/client injection, native credential lifecycles, AWS restart semantics, subprocess inheritance, observability capture, Jira adapter behavior, Pydantic secret masking, keyring trade-offs, and context-local state cite R1–R23. Current-Rebar and experiment claims cite the durable `relevant-liberal-xantus` research record and identify the audited probes. The draft intentionally does not treat point-in-time stars as architectural merit.\n\nResolution made during critique: clarified that selected provider/model policy may enter a non-secret fingerprint while live provider capability objects may not; this removes an ambiguous use of “provider.”\n\n## 2. Internal inconsistencies\n\nNo unresolved inconsistency was found.\n\n- The snapshot is immutable/non-secret; live provider capabilities and provider-specific static carriers exist only beside it in runtime bindings.\n- Static restart/redeploy rotation is explicitly distinct from SDK-native in-process refresh.\n- Rebar constructs/owns the official client while the injected native auth/session capability retains its upstream/caller lifecycle.\n- Op-cert requires exactly one startup key source, validates/copies it once, and does not promise hot reload.\n- ADR status is Proposed and `docs/architecture.md` explicitly says the migration is not implemented.\n\n## 3. Missing constraints\n\nNo unresolved constraint was found after these additions:\n\n- Inline `REBAR_OPCERT_PRIVATE_KEY` is documented as a compatibility fallback whose bytes may remain visible through process/container inspection; file input is preferred and unrelated children remove the exact inline name.\n- Non-secret path/profile references are recognized as capability-bearing: they are propagation-limited, excluded from fingerprints, and dereferenced only at the owning seam.\n- Pydantic wrappers are defense in depth, not encryption/leakage proof.\n- Targeted sibling filtering is not a sandbox; deployment scoping remains primary.\n- Raw credentials and stronger server-identity selection are excluded from CLI/MCP inputs.\n- Arbitrary constructed-client/runner injection and general secret acquisition remain separate future decisions.\n\n## 4. Conflicts with documented architectural decisions\n\nNo unresolved conflict was found.\n\n- ADR 0008 remains the deployment authority for fail-fast SSM materialization; ADR 0098 clarifies that regenerating a source alone does not rebind a running process.\n- ADR 0049 is amended only at application/runtime key retrieval and global composition. Its Ed25519/DSSE protocol, environment attribution, trust roots/eras, authoritative fetch, producer, envelope persistence, SSM-at-rest choice, and limitations remain authoritative.\n- ADRs 0014 and 0050 retain webhook/MCP auth semantics; ADRs 0041 and 0046 bound redaction and assurance claims.\n- ADR 0059 retains the provider registry/session and Rebar transport policy; ADR 0083 retains the vendor-neutral adapter boundary.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `python scripts/gen_adr_index.py --check`: PASS\n- `python scripts/check_adr_numbers.py`: PASS\n- `python scripts/check_docs_index.py`: PASS\n- `git diff --check`: PASS\n- Worktree-venv `make lint`: PASS\n- Placeholder/conflict-marker search: no findings\n- Commit trailer verification: PASS\n\nDraft critique is complete. The change remains WIP/DRAFT. Per the ADR lifecycle, HOLD and implementation decomposition require explicit maintainer approval; no implementation work has started.","timestamp":1786991987226402001}],"created_at":1786991145438506001,"creation_channel":"cli","deps":[{"link_uuid":"a20a703e-6acc-4faa-b6ad-0fcb2df2f427","relation":"discovered_from","target_id":"2704-39ae-16f5-4e96"},{"link_uuid":"36e62a2b-9d31-480b-a205-5c75886ce403","relation":"blocks","target_id":"e215-ab23-ed6a-4791"},{"link_uuid":"a80fa9a1-ec50-49ff-938f-ebe7cde8bc7a","relation":"relates_to","target_id":"e24b-245e-fec2-4833"}],"description":"## Context\nRecord the approved RP-04 architecture before implementation. The completed research and probes live on `relevant-liberal-xantus` and in `.joe-janitor/rp04-secrets-research.md`, `.joe-janitor/rp04-composition-readback.md`, and `.joe-janitor/rp04-adr-research-critique.md`. This child is documentation-only and must not start RP-04 implementation.\n\n## Deliverable\nCreate ADR 0098, `docs/adr/0098-operation-scoped-config-and-provider-composition.md`, using this repository's ADR format and generator. The ADR records one coherent decision: Rebar composes immutable non-secret operation configuration with separately owned provider-auth/runtime bindings at application boundaries. The slug deliberately avoids the generic word `bindings`, which the generated index classifies under the reconciler workstream before it sees `config`; this keeps the application-wide decision in the Config & CLI index section.\n\n## Plan\n1. Work from a fresh worktree at current `origin/main`; reserve ADR 0098 with the generated `.numbers/0098` marker.\n2. Cite the completed primary-source and maintained-OSS research for Pydantic/PydanticAI, OpenAI, Anthropic, boto3/AWS, Git credential helpers/SSH, Jira integrations, CI secret injection, subprocess environments, and Rebar's current probes. Claims about dependency behavior must point to the exact source consulted.\n3. Describe the current split authorities, trust boundaries, behavioral regressions, and why configuration and authentication are related at composition time but remain separate data/capability models.\n4. Record the approved decisions: operation snapshot precedence/root rules and lifecycle; CLI/MCP/Python/service composition ownership; subsystem projections; provider-native pre-client auth/session injection into Rebar-owned factories; provider-specific `SecretStr`/`SecretBytes` static carriers; no universal secret broker/keyring/cloud-secret retrieval; subprocess environment rules without global mutation; restart/redeploy static rotation; SDK-owned refresh; fail-closed decision-bearing auth; and the optional observability exception.\n5. Record op-cert startup composition: exactly one path or multiline value, validation before service work, process-owned 0700/0600 copy, context-local signer/principal/push policy, no application runtime SSM/boto3/region coupling, and deployment freedom to retain SSM-at-rest before injection. Amend only ADR 0049's per-job retrieval/composition detail; preserve its algorithm, trust-root/era, producer, and deployment storage choices.\n6. Explain alternatives and rejection grounds: current ambient/per-consumer loading; one secret-provider/string lookup; application-owned SSM/keyring layer; aggressively restricted child environments; global `os.environ` overlays; public constructed-client/runner replacement; per-job op-cert retrieval; and hot rotation owned by Rebar.\n7. State security limits/non-goals, migration and rollback, compatibility effects, explicit behavior changes, observability/provenance constraints, and implementation verification obligations. Relate ADRs 0008, 0014, 0041, 0046, 0049, 0050, 0059, and 0083 without claiming to supersede their unaffected decisions.\n8. Regenerate `docs/adr/README.md` and `.numbers/0098`; make the minimal `docs/architecture.md` update appropriate for a proposed decision under this project's conventions.\n9. Run ADR-number/index/docs checks. Then perform and publish the required numbered Draft Critique: uncited claims, internal inconsistencies, missing constraints, and conflicts with existing ADRs. Resolve any mechanical/document defects, but stop before HOLD/MERGE and request explicit maintainer approval.\n\n## Verification\n- `python scripts/gen_adr_index.py --check`\n- `python scripts/check_adr_numbers.py`\n- The repository's documentation/lint checks applicable to changed files.\n- Deterministic searches confirm no placeholder sections or merge-conflict markers in the changed ADR artifacts.\n- Read the rendered diff against the approved research and every related ADR named above.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] ADR 0098 exists in project-native format and accurately records the approved operation-snapshot, provider-auth/runtime-binding, subprocess, rotation, failure, observability, and op-cert decisions.\n- [ ] Every external dependency/OSS behavior claim has a specific primary or repository research citation; Rebar probe claims cite the recovery artifacts or durable research ticket.\n- [ ] Alternatives, consequences, security limitations/non-goals, explicit behavior changes, migration, rollback, and post-cutover compatibility are concrete enough that implementation stories need not invent architectural policy.\n- [ ] The ADR narrows ADR 0049 only at the per-job retrieval/composition seam and explicitly preserves all unaffected decisions; relationships to ADRs 0008, 0014, 0041, 0046, 0050, 0059, and 0083 are consistent.\n- [ ] `docs/adr/README.md` and `docs/adr/.numbers/0098` are generator-produced, collision-free, and pass their checks; `docs/architecture.md` points readers to the proposed decision without overstating implementation status.\n- [ ] The numbered Draft Critique is recorded on this ticket and identifies or explicitly clears uncited claims, inconsistencies, missing constraints, and ADR conflicts.\n- [ ] No RP-04 production code or implementation child stories are created, and the change remains in DRAFT/HOLD pending explicit maintainer authorization.","env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","file_impact":[{"path":"docs/adr/0098-operation-scoped-config-and-provider-composition.md","reason":"New RP-04 architecture decision record grounded in approved research."},{"path":"docs/adr/.numbers/0098","reason":"Generated collision-proof reservation marker for ADR 0098."},{"path":"docs/adr/README.md","reason":"Generated ADR index entry."},{"path":"docs/archi